In C, the most common way to return an error is by using the `errno` variable, which is a global variable that holds the error code. When a function detects an error, it can set the appropriate value in `errno` and optionally return a special value such as `-1` or `NULL` to indicate that an error occurred. For example, the `open()` function in the C standard library sets `errno` to indicate why the file could not be opened and returns `-1` to indicate an error.

Another common approach is to use a function that returns an error code, such as `errno`, and a pointer to a custom error structure. This way, the caller can check the error code and the custom error structure to understand the nature of the error. For instance, the `gethostbyname()` function returns a pointer to a `struct hostent` that contains information about the host, or `NULL` if an error occurs, and sets `errno` accordingly.

In some cases, you might also use a union or a struct to encapsulate the error code and additional error information in a single return value. This can be particularly useful when the error information is complex and needs to be passed back to the caller in a structured manner.

Here's an example of how you might implement a function that returns an error using a union:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef union {
    int error_code;
    void *data;
} error_union;

error_union error_function() {
    error_union result;
    // Simulate an error
    result.error_code = -1;
    return result;
}

int main() {
    error_union error_result = error_function();
    if (error_result.error_code == -1) {
        printf("An error occurred.\n");
        // Handle the error appropriately
    } else {
        printf("No error occurred.\n");
        // Use the data returned by the function
        printf("Data: %p\n", error_result.data);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `error_function` returns a union that contains either an error code or a pointer to some data. The `main` function checks the error code and handles the error accordingly.

To create a new line within a cell in a LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. Simply place `\\` at the end of the line where you want the new line to start. Here's an example:

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
        \hline
        First Column & Second Column \\ \hline
        First row, first column & \textbf{Second row, first column} \\ 
        First row, second column & \textbf{Second row, second column} \\ \hline
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Example Table with Multi-line Cells}
    \label{tab:example}
\end{table}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to create a new line within the "Second row, first column" cell. This will ensure that the text wraps to a new line when it reaches the end of the cell.

