In C++, you can use the `std::tie` function from the `<utility>` header to run the same code inline on multiple named variables. This is particularly useful when you want to swap values or pass multiple variables by reference to a function. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <utility>
#include <iostream>

void swap(int& a, int& b) {
    std::tie(a, b) = std::tie(b, a);
}

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    std::cout << "Before swap: x = " << x << ", y = " << y << std::endl;
    swap(x, y);
    std::cout << "After swap: x = " << x << ", y = " << y << std::endl;
    return 0;
